WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.399 --> 00:00:03.980
If you watched Black Mirror's interactive
movie Bandersnatch and wondered where you'd

00:00:03.980 --> 00:00:07.400
seen the actor playing Colin before, you were
far from alone.

00:00:07.990 --> 00:00:12.080
Here's what Will Poulter's been up to since
making his screen debut, and where you might

00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:13.640
have watched his work along the way.

00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:20.000
At the age of 14, Poulter played Lee Carter,
the lead role in 2007's Son of Rambow, directed

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:21.340
and written by Garth Jennings.

00:00:21.560 --> 00:00:25.340
A coming-of-age story about two young boys
and their attempt to remake the Rambo film

00:00:25.349 --> 00:00:29.840
First Blood, the film premiered to positive
reviews, especially for Poulter and his co-star

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:30.840
Bill Milner.

00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:35.500
"If you don't tell us where they're hiding,
then I have no choice but to shoot you and

00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:36.340
your cat!

00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:38.700
Do you have any last requests?"

00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:44.800
Soon after Rambow, Poulter booked a television
series in England called School of Comedy,

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:46.440
which began airing in 2008.

00:00:46.730 --> 00:00:50.340
Previously a youth-based sketch show based
out of Poulter's school, it premiered at the

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:55.300
Edinburgh Festival Fringe and was soon picked
up by E4, where it ran for two years.

00:00:55.480 --> 00:00:58.560
"The better they get, the cockier they get,
and who likes a cocky kid?

00:00:58.560 --> 00:00:59.560
NOT I!"

00:01:00.220 --> 00:01:04.700
2009 saw Poulter's biggest break yet — he
was cast in a featured role in the third film

00:01:04.700 --> 00:01:07.040
based on C.S. Lewis' Chronicles of Narnia.

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:12.240
Poulter's bookish and irritating character,
Eustace Scrubb, debuted in 2010's Voyage of

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:13.080
the Dawn Treader.

00:01:13.570 --> 00:01:17.880
The film performed well at the box office,
but didn't fare as well critically, with a

00:01:17.890 --> 00:01:19.770
50% average on Rotten Tomatoes.

00:01:19.770 --> 00:01:24.250
However, some critics singled out Poulter's
performance, with Luke Y. Thompson from E!

00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:24.740
saying that,

00:01:24.740 --> 00:01:29.580
"Poulter's turn as Eustace injects a welcome
note of comedic cynicism into the sea of sentimentality."

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:34.640
After his blockbuster year in 2010, Poulter
took on a smaller project, appearing in the

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:36.900
2011 British independent film Wild Bill.

00:01:37.540 --> 00:01:42.060
Directed by Dexter Fletcher, the crime comedy
tells the story of the titular Bill, who has

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:45.580
recently been released from prison after serving
under a vicious drug lord.

00:01:46.220 --> 00:01:49.840
Poulter took on the role of the Bill's 15-year-old
son who has dropped out of school and is now

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:51.420
working on a construction site.

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:55.600
Though the film was certainly a smaller production
than the Chronicles of Narnia, it fared much

00:01:55.600 --> 00:01:59.160
better critically, with a 100% fresh rating
on Rotten Tomatoes.

00:01:59.860 --> 00:02:04.760
In 2013, Poulter appeared in We're the Millers,
a big-budget Hollywood comedy starring Jennifer

00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:06.480
Aniston and Jason Sudeikis.

00:02:06.780 --> 00:02:11.060
Sudeikis plays a low-level pot dealer forced
to transport drugs across the U.S. border

00:02:11.060 --> 00:02:12.020
and into Mexico.

00:02:12.430 --> 00:02:16.500
Figuring he can fly under the radar if he
looks like an average Joe, he enlists Aniston

00:02:16.520 --> 00:02:20.020
to pose as his wife and two teenage runaways
to pose as his children.

00:02:20.260 --> 00:02:25.019
Poulter plays the socially awkward Kenny,
showing off his broad comedy chops, as well

00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:26.579
as his impeccable American accent.

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:31.840
"So Casey, I guess it's nice for you to get
your haircut, I mean, being homeless and all."

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:35.489
The film was successful at the box office,
and though critical response was tepid at

00:02:35.489 --> 00:02:40.080
best, Poulter's career was given a jump start,
with many critics singling him out as a highlight.

00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:45.700
In 2014, Poulter appeared as Gally in the
film adaptation of The Maze Runner, a popular

00:02:45.700 --> 00:02:47.280
series of young adult novels.

00:02:47.540 --> 00:02:51.600
Poulter described the film as a "turning point"
for his career, and it was yet another blockbuster

00:02:51.609 --> 00:02:55.599
notch to add to his belt, with an excellent
box office turnout in the United States and

00:02:55.600 --> 00:02:57.100
an even better one abroad.

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:00.600
Poulter returned to his role in 2018's Maze
Runner: The Death Cure.

00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:05.860
Poulter's next project was another indie — Glassland,
written and directed by Gerard Barrett.

00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:10.700
The film stars Jack Reynor as John, who must
help his troubled mother, played by Toni Collette,

00:03:10.700 --> 00:03:13.080
as she struggles with severe alcoholism.

00:03:13.329 --> 00:03:17.300
Poulter plays John's friend Shane, who lives
with his mother as well, but who has a much

00:03:17.300 --> 00:03:19.780
angrier and much more fraught relationship
with her.

00:03:20.060 --> 00:03:24.440
Glassland premiered to positive reviews, and
Poulter was once again identified as a standout.

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:29.980
In 2015, Alejandro G. Iñárritu followed
up his Best Picture winner Birdman with the

00:03:29.980 --> 00:03:31.580
stark western drama The Revenant.

00:03:31.989 --> 00:03:36.220
The film, which focuses on Leonardo DiCaprio's
Hugh Glass struggling to survive on the frozen

00:03:36.230 --> 00:03:41.189
frontier after he's abandoned by his group
of fellow trappers, was highly lauded, earning

00:03:41.189 --> 00:03:46.030
numerous awards and nominations, and brought
in more than $530 million at the worldwide

00:03:46.030 --> 00:03:46.940
box office.

00:03:47.340 --> 00:03:51.279
Poulter played Jim Bridger, who remains loyal
to Glass even though he is repeatedly tricked

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:53.880
by the other trappers as they try to leave
him for dead.

00:03:54.320 --> 00:03:58.300
Some critics described his part as the most
morally difficult in the entire film, and

00:03:58.309 --> 00:04:02.339
Poulter himself described the difficult shooting
experience as pushing him to the absolute

00:04:02.340 --> 00:04:02.840
limit.

00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:05.540
But in terms of prestige, it certainly paid
off.

00:04:06.380 --> 00:04:11.459
In 2017, Poulter played sadistic policeman
Philip Krauss in Kathryn Bigelow's Detroit,

00:04:11.460 --> 00:04:14.380
which tells the story of 1967's 12th Street
Riots.

00:04:15.069 --> 00:04:18.760
Krauss kills a suspected looter and spends
the remainder of the film framing innocent

00:04:18.760 --> 00:04:20.340
people for crimes that he committed.

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:24.580
Poulter's performance received numerous accolades,
and some critics even believed he could earn

00:04:24.580 --> 00:04:27.680
an Oscar nomination for his difficult, polarizing
role.

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:33.080
Before ending 2018 with a bang in Bandersnatch,
Poulter starred in The Little Stranger, based

00:04:33.080 --> 00:04:35.060
on Sarah Waters' novel of the same name.

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:38.040
Poulter plays a disfigured war veteran haunted
by his past.

00:04:38.740 --> 00:04:40.900
The Little Stranger was met with mixed reviews.

00:04:40.900 --> 00:04:45.700
But considering the accolades and blockbusters
he's racked up in such a short career already,

00:04:45.700 --> 00:04:47.780
we'll call this one a minor exception.

